    "['set protocols ospf area [area_id]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'router ospf 1', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'router ospf 1', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the translation of the Juniper OSPF configuration commands to Cisco IOS syntax:\\n\\n##\\nrouter ospf 1\\n network 4.4.4.4 0.0.0.0 area 0\\n network 10.4.1.0 0.0.0.255 area 0\\n passive-interface Loopback0\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n1. In Cisco, OSPF is enabled with a process ID (I used 1 here)\\n2. The loopback interface (lo0.1 with 4.4.4.4/32 in Juniper) is translated to:\\n   - Network statement covering the exact address with wildcard mask\\n   - Marked as passive\\n3. The ge-2/0/0.0 interface (10.4.1.1/24) is translated to a network statement covering the subnet\\n\\nNote: Cisco doesn't have direct interface-level passive configuration in OSPF like Juniper - the passive statement is applied globally to the interface under the OSPF process.\", 'trans_template': 'router ospf 1'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'network 4.4.4.4 0.0.0.0 area 0', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'network 4.4.4.4 0.0.0.0 area 0', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the translation of the Juniper OSPF configuration commands to Cisco IOS syntax:\\n\\n##\\nrouter ospf 1\\n network 4.4.4.4 0.0.0.0 area 0\\n network 10.4.1.0 0.0.0.255 area 0\\n passive-interface Loopback0\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n1. In Cisco, OSPF is enabled with a process ID (I used 1 here)\\n2. The loopback interface (lo0.1 with 4.4.4.4/32 in Juniper) is translated to:\\n   - Network statement covering the exact address with wildcard mask\\n   - Marked as passive\\n3. The ge-2/0/0.0 interface (10.4.1.1/24) is translated to a network statement covering the subnet\\n\\nNote: Cisco doesn't have direct interface-level passive configuration in OSPF like Juniper - the passive statement is applied globally to the interface under the OSPF process.\", 'trans_template': 'network 4.4.4.4 0.0.0.0 area 0'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'ne